# 07 — Explore SimdJson with Livebook

This interactive tutorial helps you choose between complete decoding, sparse
selection, and bounded file streaming. Run the cells from top to bottom. The
examples create temporary JSON files on your machine and remove them in the
last section.

## Setup

When this notebook runs outside the SimdJson repository, the first cell
installs the published package. Inside the repository it reuses the project
dependency already on the code path.

```elixir
unless Code.ensure_loaded?(SimdJson) do
  Mix.install([{:simd_json, "~> 1.0"}])
end

:ok
```

## Decode a complete document

Use `decode/1` when you need the entire JSON value as ordinary Elixir terms.

```elixir
json = ~s({"customer":{"id":42,"name":"Ada"},"active":true,"tags":["trial","north"]})

{:ok, decoded} = SimdJson.decode(json)
decoded
```

Complete decoding materializes the complete tree. For conventional documents
where you need most values, Jason is generally faster according to the
checked-in eager-decode benchmark.

## Select only the fields you need

Use `select/2` when the source is already a binary but the result needs only a
few scalar values.

```elixir
{:ok, selected} =
  SimdJson.select(json,
    customer_id: ["customer", "id"],
    customer_name: ["customer", "name"],
    active: ["active"]
  )

selected
```

Only selected scalars cross into the result map. The entire JSON input is
still validated.

## Select directly from a file

`select_file/2` passes a validated path through the BEAM boundary instead of
requiring `File.read/1` first.

```elixir
workspace = Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "simd_json_livebook_#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}")
File.mkdir_p!(workspace)

document_path = Path.join(workspace, "customer.json")
File.write!(document_path, json)

SimdJson.select_file(document_path,
  id: ["customer", "id"],
  name: ["customer", "name"]
)
```

## Stream selected fields from rows

The next cell creates an NDJSON fixture. Each line is one JSON document.

```elixir
events_path = Path.join(workspace, "events.ndjson")

events =
  1..10_000
  |> Stream.map(fn id ->
    ~s({"id":#{id},"kind":"#{if rem(id, 2) == 0, do: "even", else: "odd"}","ignored":"payload-#{id}"})
  end)
  |> Enum.intersperse("\n")

File.write!(events_path, events)
File.stat!(events_path).size
```

`stream_file/2` returns a lazy Enumerable. `Enum.take/2` demands only enough
batches to produce the requested rows.

```elixir
stream =
  SimdJson.stream_file(events_path,
    format: :ndjson,
    fields: [id: ["id"], kind: ["kind"]],
    batch_size: 128,
    max_batch_bytes: 1_048_576
  )

Enum.take(stream, 5)
```

Now consume every projected row without constructing a complete decoded list:

```elixir
summary =
  SimdJson.stream_file(events_path,
    format: :ndjson,
    fields: [id: ["id"], kind: ["kind"]],
    batch_size: 1_000,
    max_batch_bytes: 8_388_608
  )
  |> Enum.reduce(%{count: 0, id_sum: 0, kinds: %{}}, fn row, acc ->
    %{
      count: acc.count + 1,
      id_sum: acc.id_sum + row.id,
      kinds: Map.update(acc.kinds, row.kind, 1, &(&1 + 1))
    }
  end)

summary
```

## Choose the right operation

| Need | Start with |
| --- | --- |
| Complete Elixir value | `decode/1` |
| A few scalar fields from a binary | `select/2` |
| A few scalar fields without `File.read/1` | `select_file/2` |
| Lazy row processing with bounded batches | `stream_file/2` |

The published benchmark results are workload-specific. Sparse selection is
the strong throughput case. Streaming emphasizes earlier delivery and lower
memory peaks, while eager full decoding generally favors Jason.

## Clean up

```elixir
File.rm_rf!(workspace)
:ok
```

Continue with the [deployment guide](06-deployment.md) before using SimdJson in
production.
